# MCP server

`grantiva mcp` starts a Model Context Protocol server on stdio so an AI agent can build, run, drive, and
visually test the app. It works against an iOS simulator or an Android emulator; the platform is resolved
like every other command (`grantiva.yml` is iOS, `grantiva-android.yml` is Android, and
`grantiva mcp --platform ios|android` chooses when both exist). See [android.md](android.md) for the
Android specifics.

The server exposes 22 tools. `tools/list` returns exactly this set; a test fails if the tool names on this page and
the registered tool names drift apart.

## Runner session

`grantiva mcp` starts without a runner session and without a config file, so an agent can list tools
and provision a device first. Only the tools marked **yes** in the **Session** column need a session:
until one exists they return an error (`isError`) naming `grantiva runner start` and
`grantiva run --keep-alive`, and they attach to it on the next call once it starts (and re-attach if it
is replaced).

Every tool, with or without a session, reports every failure as an `isError` result whose text starts
with `Error: ` exactly once, never as a JSON-RPC error: a missing simulator or AVD, a refused delete, a
failed build (`Error: Build FAILED`), a missing config file, a VRT command that fails, an unknown tool
name (`Error: Unknown tool: <name>`). JSON-RPC errors are left for protocol faults (an unknown method, a
malformed request). A `tools/call` cancelled with `notifications/cancelled` gets no response at all,
whether the cancel arrives while the tool runs or right behind the request; a build in progress is
stopped (its `xcodebuild` or Gradle process is terminated). A cancel for an unknown or finished request
changes nothing.

The texts read as follows. With no session, a session tool says
``Error: No active runner session at <project>/.grantiva/session.json. Start one with `grantiva runner start` or `grantiva run --keep-alive` in <project>.``
(adding how many keep-alive sessions from another directory or platform were ignored, when there
were any). A failure caused by an argument, such as a simulator or AVD that does not exist, an AVD
that is still running, or a required argument that is missing, carries the label `Invalid argument: `
after `Error: `, for example `Error: Invalid argument: Simulator not found: "no-such-sim"`,
``Error: Invalid argument: AVD "x" is running as emulator-5554. Run `grantiva emulator teardown --serial emulator-5554` first.``
or `Error: Invalid argument: 'name' is required.` A no-session text never carries the label (a session
tool reports the missing session before it looks at its arguments). The resource reads below report the
same conditions without that label.

### A session that is starting
OVERVIEW: Start the Grantiva MCP server for AI agent integration.

The server starts without a runner session or a config file. Device tools
(grantiva_tap, grantiva_swipe, grantiva_type, grantiva_screenshot,
grantiva_a11y_*, grantiva_script) need a runner session and return an error
until one exists (while one is starting, an error saying so and to retry in a
few seconds); they pick it up on the next call. The session is the project's
.grantiva/session.json (grantiva runner start), or a grantiva run --keep-alive
or runner start session started from the same project directory for the same
platform. Sessions from other projects or the other platform are never used.
Simulator, emulator, build, context, and VRT tools work without a session.

USAGE: grantiva mcp [--project-dir <project-dir>] [--platform <platform>]

OPTIONS:
  --project-dir <project-dir>
                          Project directory (default: the current directory).
                          Its grantiva.yml or grantiva-android.yml and runner
                          session are used when present.
  --platform <platform>   Target platform: ios or android. Defaults to
                          whichever of grantiva.yml / grantiva-android.yml
                          exists, else the project files in this directory.
                          GRANTIVA_PLATFORM also sets it. (values: ios, android)
  --version               Show the version.
  -h, --help              Show help information.

emulator's port 6790 (`adb forward tcp:0 tcp:6790`) for the duration of the command and
reads the session the runner holds. `--udid <serial>` picks a session when several are
live. `runner dump-hierarchy` reads the same tree and prints it as a tree, JSON, or XML.

## Recording

    grantiva record --duration 5 --frames-at 0,1000,3000

Records with `screenrecord` to `.grantiva/recordings/recording.mp4` and extracts frames
as PNGs. Android caps a recording at 180 seconds; longer durations are refused.
`screenrecord` writes a frame only when the screen changes; Grantiva holds the last frame
until `--duration`, so a static screen still yields a video of the requested length and a PNG
for every timestamp (`actualMilliseconds` is the frame on screen at that moment). When the
last frame was on screen for more than half of the requested duration (the screen last changed
before the halfway point), `record` prints a note on stderr naming when that frame appears:
expected for an idle screen, but a device that disconnected mid-recording looks the same. This
holds whether Grantiva stretched the last frame or `screenrecord` already gave it a long sample
(the note then says so and gives the track's length).
When `screenrecord` stops it gives its last frame the previous frame's duration, so a recording
can run past `--duration`; Grantiva then trims that last frame so the video ends at `--duration`.
A last frame that itself starts after `--duration` is left as recorded (the `Held frame:` line
